@* Blazor's unhandled-error bar.

   The framework owns all of the behaviour. `blazor.web.js` finds this element by
   id and shows it by setting an inline `style.display="block"`, and the dismiss
   handler is its too — there is deliberately no @@onclick here and nothing to
   wire up, which is why none of this can be tested from C#.

   `T155`: this markup used to be written out twice, once in MainLayout and once
   in EmptyLayout, and only MainLayout had a stylesheet. Scoped CSS compiles to
   `#blazor-error-ui[b-…]` and that attribute is stamped only on the markup of the
   component the stylesheet belongs to — so under EmptyLayout nothing matched, the
   `display: none` never applied, and the sign-in, setup and access-denied pages
   each rendered a permanent "An unhandled error has occurred." below the fold
   with a 🗙 that looked inert. Nothing had failed.

   It lives here, rendered once from App.razor, rather than being fixed by moving
   the rules into the global stylesheet. The duplicated markup was the actual
   cause: a third layout would have forgotten the div entirely and had no banner
   at all. ReconnectModal is the same shape for the same reason — a
   framework-recognised element by id, its own scoped CSS, rendered once. *@

<div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
    An unhandled error has occurred.
    <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
    <span class="dismiss">🗙</span>
</div>
